ntfs-3g (ntfs-macos) patch 0014: per-handle readdir snapshot with offsets in the FUSE glue (all platforms)

Rationale (S-PAR-01 EINVAL from os.listdir, investigation 2026-11-22, ADR-027): upstream
ntfs_fuse_readdir in src/ntfs-3g.c ignores the offset, lists the whole directory from position 0
and passes offset 0 to the filler for every entry. libfuse's high-level API then keeps the whole
listing in a buffer per directory handle and answers each FUSE_READDIR with contents + off, clipped
only to the buffer length, so a reply can end inside a fuse_dirent; a READDIR with offset 0 refills
that buffer. The Linux kernel and the macFUSE kext ignore a cut trailing record. The macFUSE 5.5.0
FSKit module does not:
  - it asks for 10240 bytes per FUSE_READDIR and fails the whole enumeration with EINVAL when the
    reply ends in a record cut after its 24-byte header. getdirentries64 with a buffer of 16 KiB or
    more, ls (fts_read: Invalid argument) and getattrlistbulk (Finder) fail on every directory whose
    listing is longer than 10240 bytes and whose cut falls inside a name (116 names of 58 bytes fail,
    115 pass);
  - it reuses one FUSE directory handle for several enumerations, so the offset-0 READDIR of one
    reader refills the buffer under another reader whose cookie (a byte offset into the old buffer)
    then points into a record: intermittent EINVAL and EIO with two or more readers and a writer in
    the same directory, and twice a crash of the module (Swift assertion in
    Request.BodySegment.managed(copy:) under Volume.enumerateDirectory), which unmounts the volume.
A minimal libfuse2 file system with the same offset-ignoring readdir shows all of this and one that
passes offsets shows none of it, so the trigger is the readdir mode, not NTFS (report to macFUSE
drafted, docs/UPSTREAM_REPORTS.md). The macFUSE kernel (kext) backend was not available and is not
covered.
Design: each open directory handle keeps a snapshot of its listing, and READDIR passes offsets.
  - opendir allocates an empty snapshot and stores it in fi->fh; releasedir frees it. Upstream
    registers opendir and releasedir only for the permission-checking builds
    (!KERNELPERMS | (POSIXACLS & !KERNELACLS)); 0014 registers them always. In those builds the
    upstream opendir runs first (access checks; a reparse plugin's opendir may set fi->fh), and a
    handle whose fi->fh a plugin set gets no snapshot and is released by the upstream release path.
    Snapshots are kept in a list so that releasedir and readdir only ever take an fi->fh that 0014
    allocated (ntfs-3g runs fuse_loop, one thread).
  - The first READDIR of a handle, and every READDIR at offset 0 (rewinddir), reads the whole
    directory once with ntfs_readdir() from position 0, through the same ntfs_fuse_filler as before
    (same entries, same filters: root self reference, metadata and hidden files under
    show_sys_files/hide_hid_files, DOS names, named-stream names; hide_dot_files and windows_names
    act only at create and rename), into the snapshot: name, inode number, mode and the entry's
    ntfs_readdir() position.
  - READDIR is then served from the snapshot. The offset of an entry is its position + 1: "." 1,
    ".." 2, then INDEX_ROOT entries (byte offset of the entry, at least 16) and INDEX_ALLOCATION
    entries (MFT record size + block offset + entry offset), so offsets increase along the snapshot
    and are never 0, which libfuse reserves for the offset-less mode. A READDIR at offset k serves
    from the first entry whose offset is above k (binary search) until the filler refuses one
    (buffer full); libfuse has not stored that entry, and the next READDIR comes with the offset of
    the last entry it took, so the refused one comes again: no loss, no duplicate.
  - A handle that only one listing uses reads an unchanged snapshot, so unlink during the
    iteration (readdir; unlink; ...; rmdir, as git and busybox rm -r do) removes every entry: the
    index compaction of ntfs_ie_delete does not move the rest (tested: Linux, and macOS without a
    concurrent lister). Entries made after the snapshot are not seen by that handle until it rewinds
    (POSIX leaves them unspecified).
  - Not guaranteed on the FSKit backend while another process lists the same directory: the module
    serves all enumerations of a directory from one FUSE handle and continues an enumeration from
    the handle's own position, not from the caller's cookie (a FUSE trace shows no READDIR carrying
    the first enumeration's offsets once the second has started). A readdir + unlink loop over 1000
    files with an ls -f or getattrlistbulk loop running beside it left 26 to 52 files in
    every run (unpatched: about 460, from EINVAL and the shared buffer). Keeping the snapshots of
    earlier listings per handle (tagging offsets with a generation) was tried and changed nothing,
    because those offsets never come back; this is a macFUSE defect (docs/UPSTREAM_REPORTS.md).
  - When a READDIR with a nonzero offset reaches a handle without a valid snapshot, a new snapshot is
    taken and served from the first entry whose position is above the offset; entries of the same
    index block can then be listed twice or not at all if the directory changed in between. Such a
    continuation reads the whole directory again, so a listing continued on k new handles costs k
    full reads (n entries each).
  - Memory: the two arrays of a snapshot (entries and names) grow by doubling and are each limited
    to 1 GiB (NTFS_FUSE_SNAP_MAX), so one open directory handle holds at most 2 GiB (about ten million
    entries); a mount holds one snapshot per open directory handle. Beyond the limit, or when
    realloc fails, READDIR answers ENOMEM. Upstream kept the same listing in libfuse's buffer,
    without a limit. The snapshot keeps its capacity until releasedir.
  - The reparse-point plugin path keeps the upstream mode (start at 0, offset 0 for every entry, the
    plugin sees the fi->fh it saw before), and a handle without a snapshot (no opendir) uses the
    upstream mode too.
Hunks:
  - src/ntfs-3g.c: the snapshot types, ntfs_fuse_snap_find/_add/_serve, ntfs_fuse_filler fills the
    snapshot when there is one, ntfs_fuse_readdir as above, ntfs_fuse_opendir_snap and
    ntfs_fuse_releasedir_snap, the two table entries, <stdint.h>.
  - libfuse-lite/fuse.c (Linux internal FUSE only; macOS uses macFUSE's libfuse): readdir_fill set
    dh->filled before the fill, and only an entry with a nonzero offset cleared it, so after the
    empty READDIR at the end of a listing every later READDIR at a nonzero offset on that handle
    got 0 bytes without asking the file system (seekdir to a telldir cookie after the end). An empty
    fill now leaves the handle unfilled. macFUSE's libfuse has the same code; see Test.
Not changed: libntfs-3g. Every listing starts at position 0, so ntfs_readdir()'s resume path is not
used; its bitmap bug when resuming inside the index allocation (it tests bit 0 of the bitmap byte
instead of bit bmp_pos & 7, for every block until the next 4 KiB chunk of the bitmap) stays
upstream and is recorded in docs/UPSTREAM_REPORTS.md. src/lowntfs-3g.c is not patched and not
shipped (patch 0003).
Cost: one ntfs_readdir() per snapshot (per listing, as before) and one binary search per READDIR;
one inode lookup per snapshot instead of per READDIR; a walk of the open directory handles of the
mount per READDIR to recognise fi->fh.
Upstream status: to report upstream as a suggestion (offset-based readdir helps every FUSE client
that does not tolerate a cut record) together with the fuse-lite fix; texts to be drafted; sending
is a human decision. The FSKit module defects are reported to macFUSE separately (draft).
Test: tests/integration/readdir (I-RDIR-01..05, Environment B; the regression check for macFUSE and
NTFS-3G updates) and S-PAR-01 (pass: 115887 operations, 0 errors, 0 mismatches). Checked on 2026-11-22, patched against unpatched:
  - Environment B: 149 unchanged directories (100 to 10000 entries, names of 8 to 120 bytes), 1937
    checks by os.listdir, ls -f, getdirentries64 (2 to 128 KiB) and getattrlistbulk (1 to 64 KiB):
    all complete in the same order (unpatched: 392 listings fail with EINVAL); readdir + unlink +
    rmdir on 100, 1000 and 10000 entries alone: empty; seekdir after the end; two descriptors
    handing off (2 and 16 KiB); growth from INDEX_ROOT only during a listing; the root directory;
    21 concurrent reader and writer cases: 0 EINVAL, 0 EIO, no module crash, no unchanged entry
    lost or repeated (unpatched: up to 4203 EINVAL per case, two crashes);
  - Environment B, readdir + unlink with a concurrent ls -f or getattrlistbulk loop: files left in
    every run (1000 entries: 36 of 36 runs, 26 to 52 left where logged; 10000: 17 of 17, 112 to 397;
    the unlink failures are ENOENT, names delivered twice;
    unpatched about 460 of 1000), with or without per-handle generations (above);
  - Environment A (Linux, fuse-lite): the same listings, unlink loop, seekdir after the end (lists
    nothing without the fuse-lite hunk), handoff, growth, root, 600 files with DOS names, 8 writers
    and 4 readers: all correct, as with pristine NTFS-3G; probe tests pass.

